
In Spring and Spring Boot, there are four main ways to put configuration, depending on what you're configuring — for example, beans, properties, or environment-specific settings.

✅ 1. Java-Based Configuration
Uses @Configuration and @Bean annotations.

Fully type-safe and purely in Java.

Example:


@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    public MyService myService() {
        return new MyServiceImpl();
    }
}


✅ 2. Annotation-Based Configuration
Uses annotations like @Component, @Service, @Repository, and @Autowired.

Beans are auto-scanned using @ComponentScan.

Example:


@Component
public class MyComponent {}

@Service
public class MyService {
    @Autowired
    private MyRepository repo;
}


✅ 3. XML-Based Configuration (Old Style)
Uses XML files like beans.xml or applicationContext.xml.

Mostly used in legacy Spring apps.

Example:


<bean id="myService" class="com.example.MyServiceImpl"/>


✅ 4. Property/Configuration Files
In Spring Boot, configuration is usually placed in:

application.properties or

application.yml

You can define:

Database properties

Server port

Logging levels

Custom app config

Example – application.properties:


server.port=8081
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb
myapp.title=My Spring Boot App
You can access them using @Value or @ConfigurationProperties:


@Value("${myapp.title}")
private String title;


✅ Bonus: Environment Variables and Command-Line Args
Spring Boot supports overriding configuration via:

Environment variables

--command-line-args

Example:


java -jar myapp.jar --server.port=9090


🧠 Summary Table
| Method                     | Used For                       | Common In      |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------ | -------------- |
| Java Config (`@Bean`)      | Defining beans                 | All apps       |
| Annotations (`@Component`) | Auto-detect and wire beans     | Spring Boot    |
| XML Config                 | Manual bean config             | Legacy apps    |
| `.properties` / `.yml`     | App-level settings             | Spring Boot    |
| Env Vars / CLI Args        | Overriding settings at runtime | DevOps, Docker |

